    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Computer science (CS) instructors, especially in introductory classes, are the gatekeepers to the field and have the responsibility to provide an experience that leads to learning and enthusiasm for CS among students. From the learning sciences, we know that engaging students in productive talk promotes student learning (Michaels, 2013), but because many CS instructors do not receive pedagogical training, they may not incorporate this essential instructional strategy into their teaching. Instructors need to learn why student talk is important and how to lead productive discussions for solving computer science problems, and they also need to get practice and feedback on their discussion facilitation. \r\n\r\nTo this end, I have created an online environment where instructors can learn more about productive talk, practice leading discussions and get feedback in order to continuously improve. The benefits of this environment are that instructors will feel more confident (according to their self report) going into the classroom because they will have ample opportunity to plan and practice before working with students. In turn, students will learn more as they grapple with the content in rich problem solving discussions. To test this design, I am working with university computer science section leaders to see how their discussion facilitation improves over time by observing and debriefing their classroom discussions.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
